{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 american biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by acid houellebecq and the wachowskis. however it was written by the wachowskis and ben mezrich who adapted mezrich's 2009 non-fiction book 'the accidental billionaires'. the film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, the co-founder of facebook, alongside andrew garfield, justin timberlake, joshua jackson, and armie hammer.\n\nthe film is set in 2003 and follows the early days of facebook at harvard university, where mark zuckerberg and his friends created the social networking site. the film explores the creation of facebook, the lawsuit that followed, and the impact it had on the lives of the people involved.\n\nthe film received widespread critical acclaim for its direction, writing, and performances. the cinematography by jeff cronkite was praised for its sleek and modern aesthetic, capturing the essence of the digital age. the score by trent reznor and atticus ross was also praised for its haunting and atmospheric sound.\n\njesse eisenberg's portrayal of mark zuckerberg was particularly notable, capturing the complexities and nuances of the character. his performance was praised for its accuracy and depth, bringing to life the character of mark zuckerberg in a way that was both compelling and relatable.\n\nthe film's portrayal of the early days of facebook was also praised for its accuracy and attention to detail. the film's writer ben mezrich was a friend of mark zuckerberg's in college and drew heavily from his experiences to create the film.\n\nthe social network was a commercial success, grossing over 225 million dollars worldwide. it also received several awards and nominations, including eight academy award nominations, including best picture and best director.\n\none of the most notable aspects of the film is its portrayal of the impact